return to main menu Arts & Humanities Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Law Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Nursing, Midwifery & Palliative Care Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Business Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Life Sciences & Medicine Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Natural, Mathematical & Engineering Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Dentistry, Oral & Craniofacial Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Social Sciences & Public Policy Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu About Navigation category. Contains links. Press enter key to expand. About Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu History Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Strategy Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Learning & Teaching Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Our People Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Work at King's Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Partnerships Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Governance, policies & procedures Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Professional & Support Services Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Diversity & Inclusion Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Financial information Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni Open search input Jobs at King’s College London Your career Navigation link How we recruit Navigation link Our benefits Navigation link Our culture Navigation link About King's Navigation link Muslim Chaplain & Advisor on Islamophobia Job id: 158647. Salary: Grade 6 (0.8FTE): £36,024.80 - £42,011.20 (including London Weighting; pro-rata). Posted: 16 September 2026. Closing date: 21 October 2026. Business unit: Dean's Office & Chaplaincy. Department: Dean's Office & Chaplaincy. Contact details: Tim Ditchfield.  tim.ditchfield@kcl.ac.uk Location: Waterloo Campus. Category: Professional & Support Services. Back to job search About us The multi-faith Chaplaincy team at King’s College London works across all five campuses of this world-renowned University, based in central and south London, which aims to deliver exceptional education and world-leading research. King’s is committed to creating positive and sustainable change in our local and global communities through outstanding education, impactful research, and genuine service to society.  Located within the Dean’s Office (see www.kcl.ac.uk/dean ), the Chaplaincy plays an important role in the field of student welfare, in collaboration with colleagues in other areas where applicable, and also provides a support service to King’s staff as needed. About the role The Muslim Chaplain and Advisor on  Islamophobia Hate is a new dual-focus role designed to provide holistic support to the significant Muslim community at King’s. Simultaneously with providing appropriate pastoral care to students and staff of all backgrounds, they will serve as an advisor to the University, providing expert guidance on identifying and addressing Islamophobia, ensuring a safe environment that balances the wellbeing of Muslim members with the University’s commitment to academic freedom and freedom of expression. The Chaplain and Advisor will provide pastoral care and community leadership, including offering a culturally competent space for Muslim individuals to discuss concerns, and supporting and facilitating Islamic religious life on campus (incorporating strategic oversight and management of the Muslim Prayer Rooms across campuses). The Chaplain and Advisor will advise the Dean and colleagues in Student Services on proactive \"inclusive adjustments\" for Muslim students and staff, particularly concerning Ramadan, Eid, and daily prayer requirements, and in acting as the primary subject matter expert for senior leadership within the University, will provide briefings for leadership and attend high-level meetings as required to address incidents or systemic concerns regarding Islamophobia sentiment. The Chaplain and Advisor will build and maintain relationships within and beyond King’s, facilitating stronger links between Muslim student/staff groups and the wider University body to promote mutual understanding and communal cohesion, and developing  partnerships with external Islamic agencies, community leaders, and relevant national bodies. The Chaplain and Advisor will work with the University’s EDI (Equality, Diversity & Inclusion) team and Student Support to source, develop, and oversee the delivery of Islamophobia hate awareness training for appropriate University audiences (such as Security, HR, and academic staff). They will also ensure that the University is equipped with the vocabulary and frameworks necessary to identify and dismantle Islamophobic tropes and discriminatory practices. This is a part time post (28 hours per week), and you will be offered an indefinite contract. About you To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria Subject Matter Expertise: Deep understanding of Islam and the diverse ways it is practiced, with the ability to provide expert guidance on addressing Islamophobia within a Higher Education context. Pastoral Excellence: Proficiency in active, confidential listening and spiritual care, with a clear understanding of professional boundaries and when to refer individuals to specialist services. Communication: Excellent spoken and written communication skills, with the ability to draft high-level briefings and engage authoritatively with senior university leadership and external stakeholders. Interpersonal & Cross-Cultural Fluency: Demonstrable experience in multi-faith working and the ability to build rapport with a broad range of students and staff of all faiths and none. Student Engagement: Proven ability to connect with a diverse student body with empathy, while offering appropriate challenge to foster personal and communal growth. Institutional Diplomacy: Ability to navigate complex issues regarding freedom of expression and academic freedom, providing nuanced advice that balances community safety with university values. Collaborative Teamwork: Proven ability to work effectively and contribute proactively as a member of a diverse team of colleagues within a fast-paced environment. Educational Delivery: Experience in, or a clear aptitude for, sourcing or delivering training and workshops aimed at increasing cultural literacy and reducing prejudice. Desirable criteria Academic/Theological Qualifications: Post-graduate training or recognised qualifications in Islamic Studies or a related field. Professional Pastoral Training: Professional accreditation or recognised training in counselling, chaplaincy, or mental health first aid. Conflict Mediation: Formal experience or qualification in conflict resolution, restorative justice, or mediation, particularly in multi-faith or highly polarised environments. Sector Expertise: A sophisticated understanding of the current regulatory and social challenges facing the UK Higher Education sector. Community Engagement: Experience in developing community-led initiatives that promote social cohesion or inter-communal dialogue. Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. Further information At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapidly, and we recognise its growing role in professional work. Applicants may use AI tools to support preparation of their application, for example to research the role or structure written responses. However, applications must reflect the applicant’s own work and experience. AI tools should not be used during interviews or assessment activities unless this has been agreed in advance as a reasonable adjustment. Further guidance on the use of AI in recruitment can be found here. We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. We welcome your thoughts on how organisations can create an inclusive environment, including one which supports free speech and the exchange of a wide range of ideas. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘ How we Recruit ’ pages. Interviews are expected to be held on 2 or 3 November 2026. This post is subject to Disclosure and Barring Service clearances. Apply now Apply now How we recruit At King’s we seek to recruit and retain skilled and engaged colleagues to deliver the competitive advantage. King’s benefits Our pay and benefits package attracts the best candidates from around the world. Creating meaningful impact Addressing urgent global challenges to shape a better world. Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link Alumni Footer navigation link Facilities Student services Footer navigation link Accommodation Footer navigation link Libraries & Collections Footer navigation link King’s IT Footer navigation link Academic Calendar Footer navigation link Discover King's News Centre Footer navigation link Events Footer navigation link Student's Union Footer navigation link Visit King's Footer navigation link Work at King's Footer navigation link King's community Footer navigation link Contact us +44 (0)20 7836 5454 King's College London Strand London WC2R 2LS United Kingdom Terms & conditions Footer navigation link Privacy policy Footer navigation link Modern slavery statement Footer navigation link Site help Footer navigation link Accessibility Footer navigation link Manage cookies Footer navigation link This website uses cookies to enhance user experience and to analyze performance and traffic on our website. We also share information about your use of our site with our social media, advertising and analytics partners. Do Not Sell My Personal Information Accept Cookies Share this link on Facebook Share this link on X Share this link on Linkedin Print Email More sharing options Share content on your favourite social media platforms Close pop-up box Muslim Chaplain & Advisor on Islamophobia | King's College London https://www.kcl.ac.uk/jobs/158647-muslim-chaplain-and-advisor-on-islamophobia Share this link on Facebook Facebook Share this link on X X Share this link on Linkedin Linkedin Share this link on Pinterest Pinterest Share this link on Tumblr Tumblr Share this link on Xing Xing Share this link on Reddit Reddit Share this link on VK VK Print Print Email Email"
