eturn to main menu Arts & Humanities Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Law Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Nursing, Midwifery & Palliative Care Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Business Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Life Sciences & Medicine Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Natural, Mathematical & Engineering Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Dentistry, Oral & Craniofacial Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Social Sciences & Public Policy Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu About Navigation category. Contains links. Press enter key to expand. About Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu History Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Strategy Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Learning & Teaching Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Our People Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Work at King's Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Partnerships Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Governance, policies & procedures Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Professional & Support Services Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Diversity & Inclusion Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Financial information Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni King's College London Job id: 159985 . Salary: £39,076 - £40,733 per annum, including London Weighting Allowance . Posted: 06 October 2026 . Closing date: 20 October 2026 . Business unit: IoPPN . Department: Neuroimaging . Contact details: Dr Ekaterina Shatalina . Ekaterina.shatalina@kcl.ac.uk Location: Denmark Hill Campus . Category: Research . Back to job search About us: King’s College London’s Institute of Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience (IoPPN) brings together research and education across mental health, psychology and neuroscience. Within the School of Neuroscience, the Department of Neuroimaging develops and applies imaging and computational approaches to understand the brain in health and disease. About the role: We are seeking one full-time Research Assistant to join Dr Ekaterina Shatalina’s team in the Department of Neuroimaging at King’s College London and help build the world’s largest pharmacological functional MRI (pharmaco-fMRI) database. People with similar psychiatric symptoms can respond very differently to the same medicine, yet treatment selection remains largely based on trial and error. To predict how the brain responds to intervention, neuroscience needs foundation models trained on large, diverse neuroimaging datasets. No single study can provide data at this scale. The lab is assembling the largest dataset in existence designed to enable foundation models for predicting brain responses to pharmacological interventions. The database will be hosted at King’s and developed through the Pharmacological Neuroimaging Network, an international consortium spanning the UK, Europe, Australia and the United States. The postholder will drive dataset creation by supporting multi-institutional data-sharing agreements, coordinating secure and timely data transfer, organising imaging data and metadata, undertaking validation and quality control, preprocessing functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging data through reproducible workflows, and contributing to analysis. Working with researchers and professional services colleagues at King’s and collaborating universities, you will help with building the database, curating, preprocessing and analysing the data and contribute to dissemination and publications. The role reports to Dr Ekaterina Shatalina and offers an opportunity to work at the intersection of artificial intelligence, neuroimaging and psychopharmacology and to develop your own international network. We recognise that early-career applicants may need further training in some elements of the role. Appropriate training and support will be provided. This is a full-time post, and you will be offered a fixed-term contract. Contract duration: 12 months, with the possibility of extending. Research staff at King’s are entitled to at least 10 days per year (pro-rata) for professional development. This entitlement, from the Concordat to Support the Career Development of Researchers , applies to Postdocs, Research Assistants, Research and Teaching Technicians, Teaching Fellows and AEP equivalent up to and including grade 7. Visit the Centre for Research Staff Development for more information. About you: To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates with the following skills and experience: Essential criteria A degree, or equivalent practical experience, in a relevant quantitative, scientific, computational, technical or health-related field, with evidence of aptitude for quantitative or computational research. Evidence of taking ownership of a project or substantial workstream, for example by planning work, managing priorities and deadlines, documenting progress and resolving problems with appropriate escalation. Demonstrated interest in frontier neurotechnology or neuropsychopharmacology, evidenced through study, employment, projects, training or independent engagement with emerging technologies. Experience of working effectively in a multidisciplinary team, with the ability to communicate clearly with collaborators. Desirable criteria Practical experience of working with and analysing MRI, functional MRI or other neuroimaging data. Experience using a programming or scripting language for data analysis, such as Python, R, MATLAB or shell scripting. Awareness of good human data-management practices, for example pseudonymisation, quality control, metadata curation, reproducibility or FAIR data principles. Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document provided at the bottom of the page. The document explains which criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. Further Information At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapidly, and we recognise its growing role in professional work. Applicants may use AI tools to support preparation of their application, for example to research the role or structure written responses. However, applications must reflect the applicant’s own work and experience. AI tools should not be used during interviews unless this has been agreed in advance as a reasonable adjustment. Further guidance on the use of AI in recruitment can be found here . We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘How we Recruit’ pages. Interview date: To be confirmed. Apply now Apply now Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link Alumni Foote