raries & Collections Navigation link in category Student Services. Press escape key to return to main menu Student's Union Navigation link in category Student Services. Press escape key to return to main menu Careers Navigation link in category Student Services. Press escape key to return to main menu Language Centre Navigation link in category Student Services. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s IT Navigation link in category Student Services. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Answers Navigation link in category Student Services. Press escape key to return to main menu Mental health and wellbeing Navigation link in category Student Services. Press escape key to return to main menu Student news Navigation link in category Student Services. Press escape key to return to main menu Research & Innovation Navigation category. Contains links. Press enter key to expand. Research & Innovation Navigation link in category Research & Innovation. Press escape key to return to main menu Explore Navigation link in category Research & Innovation. Press escape key to return to main menu Impact Navigation link in category Research & Innovation. Press escape key to return to main menu Research Environment Navigation link in category Research & Innovation. Press escape key to return to main menu Funding Navigation link in category Research & Innovation. Press escape key to return to main menu Facilities Navigation link in category Research & Innovation. Press escape key to return to main menu Collaborate with King's Navigation link in category Research & Innovation. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Health Partners Navigation link in category Research & Innovation. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Doctoral College Navigation link in category Research & Innovation. Press escape key to return to main menu Our faculties Navigation category. Contains links. Press enter key to expand. Our faculties Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Arts & Humanities Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Law Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Nursing, Midwifery & Palliative Care Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Business Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Life Sciences & Medicine Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Natural, Mathematical & Engineering Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Dentistry, Oral & Craniofacial Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Social Sciences & Public Policy Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu About Navigation category. Contains links. Press enter key to expand. About Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu History Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Strategy Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Learning & Teaching Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Our People Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Work at King's Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Partnerships Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Governance, policies & procedures Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Professional & Support Services Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Diversity & Inclusion Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Financial information Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni King's College London Job id: 160085 . Salary: £38,304 – £45,026  per annum, including London Weighting Allowance . Posted: 13 October 2026 . Closing date: 25 October 2026 . Business unit: Faculty of Life Sciences & Medicine . Department: Medical Education . Contact details: Colin Butchers . Colin.butchers@kcl.ac.uk Location: Guy's Campus . Category: Research . Back to job search About You The GKT School of Medical Education aims to educate our students to be compassionate, critical thinkers who endeavour to make a positive difference across society. The role of the Medical Education Fellow (Clinical Skills) is to meet the evolving needs of the medical curriculum, using evidence based pedagogical approaches to deliver a range of teaching from clinical skills, full patient simulation and seminar-based workshops mapped to the GMC MLA graduate outcomes. About The Role The role of the Clinical Skills Medical Education Fellow is to meet the evolving needs of clinical skills and simulation-based teaching in Undergraduate Medical Education at Kings. The post holder will be a post foundation year Doctor who will be involved in the delivery of a range of clinical practice teaching and simulation. There will be opportunities to support the design and development of teaching, simulation and e-learning resources. The role is designed to provide continuity of educational and operational support to the Head of Clinical Skills. Key working days will be Wednesdays and Thursdays to ensure delivery of key teaching, with the full-time equivalent hours of 1.5 days a week (0.3FTE), achieved over a two week rotation of one or two days a week. This will require the post holder to take a scholarly approach to the design, delivery & evaluation of simulation-based education and  face to face clinical practice skills teaching. You will be part of a dynamic team who work creatively and proactively to improve the student learning experience on campus and across the clinical sites. There are range of expected roles, primarily focusing on teaching foundation clinical skills in a first-year module (Values Based Clinical Practice), small group teaching on Wednesdays for years 2 and 3 (workshops relating to clinical documentation and prescribing), and providing cover for base based discussion (CBD) workshops. Other duties will include facilitating medium and high-fidelity simulation and workshops in the Interprofessional Education (IPE) programme. The strand of IPE teaching provides a range of learning focusing on team-based practice with seminars on (1) Promoting Patient Safety, (2) Pain Assessment and Management, (3) Prevention of Medication Errors. The IPE programme includes Full Patient Simulation (FPS), which focuses on collaborative practice and human factors skills. Another role is the support of the evolving curriculum in developing e-learning content, as well as supporting assessment (OSCE examining) and marking student submissions in the year 1 module. The post holder will also provide support in the design and delivery of campus-based teaching for Wednesday Small Group and Case Based Learning, that is mapped to the MLA and the GKT graduate outcomes. This teaching is to Stage 2 (years 2 & 3) students at the Guys and Kings campuses on Wednesdays. A key role will be to design e-learning resources on university website (KEATS) for self-directed learning relating to the prescribing workshop content as well as supporting the delivery of peer assisted learning (P.A.L) programme using senior students to facilitate Stage 1 clinical skills. This is a part-time post (10.5 hours per week), and you will be offered a 1 year fixed term contract About You To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria Full GMC registration with valid license to practice Completed foundation year programme Broad range of relevant health professional clinical skills Excellent verbal and written communication skills Excellent team worker Teaching within the healthcare context. Desirable criteria Post Graduate Certification in Clinical Education (PGCert) Design and/or Facilitation of Simulation Based Education Knowledge / teaching of human factors skills in healthcare Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. Further Information At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapidly, and we recognise its growing role in professional work. Applicants may use AI tools to support preparation of their application, for example to research the role or structure written responses. However, applications must reflect the applicant’s own work and experience. AI tools should not be used during interviews or assessment activities unless this has been agreed in advance as a reasonable adjustment. Further guidance on the use of AI in recruitment can be found here. We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘How we Recruit ’ pages. Apply now Apply now Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link Alumni Footer navigation link Facilities Student services Footer navigation link Accommodation Footer navigation link L