Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Arts & Humanities Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Law Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Nursing, Midwifery & Palliative Care Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Business Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Life Sciences & Medicine Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Natural, Mathematical & Engineering Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Dentistry, Oral & Craniofacial Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Social Sciences & Public Policy Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu About Navigation category. Contains links. Press enter key to expand. About Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu History Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Strategy Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Learning & Teaching Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Our People Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Work at King's Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Partnerships Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Governance, policies & procedures Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Professional & Support Services Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Diversity & Inclusion Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Financial information Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni Open search input Jobs at King’s College London Your career Navigation link How we recruit Navigation link Our benefits Navigation link Our culture Navigation link About King's Navigation link Communications Manager - Centre for Doctoral Training in Safe and Trusted AI Job id: 161245 . Salary: £45,031 - £52,514 per annum, including London weighting . Posted: 20 October 2026 . Closing date: 03 November 2026 . Business unit: Natural, Mathematical & Engineering Sci . Department: NMES Faculty Office . Contact details: Sarah Werts . Sarah.werts@kcl.ac.uk Location: Strand Campus . Category: Professional & Support Services . Back to job search About us The UKRI Centre for Doctoral Training in Safe and Trusted Artificial Intelligence (STAI CDT), is led by King’s College London in collaboration with Imperial College London and was established in 2019 to equip PhD students with the skills and experience to develop AI that is safe, trusted, and responsible. About the role This role comes at an exciting time for the CDT, as in the next few years it will evolve from the STAI CDT to the Safe, Trusted and Responsible AI (STaR-AI) doctoral programme, which will develop PhD students who understand the technical challenges of building safe and trustworthy AI, can engage critically with its human and societal implications, and can work confidently across disciplines to ensure AI technologies have positive impact. The role is an opportunity for an experienced Communications Manager to deliver strategic and operational communications support to raise the profile of the CDT and increase engagement with external partners. The role holder will be responsible for developing the communications strategy for the evolution from STAI to STaR-AI, and for managing the CDT’s digital channels, growing engagement rates, followers and reach amongst key audiences. This is a ​full-time​ post - ​100​% full time equivalent. This post will be offered on a fixed-term contract for one year. About you To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria 1.      Degree qualification or equivalent relevant professional experience 2.      A proactive approach to work, knowing when to work independently and flexibly but also when best to work as part of a team. 3.      Able to think strategically and develop strategic direction 4.      Experience and knowledge of managing and optimising websites and social media channels 5.      Excellent editorial skills and attention to detail 6.      Ability to write accessible and engaging copy for the web and different social media channels. 7.      Strong organisational skills and ability to manage competing priorities and multiple work-streams independently 8.      Excellent interpersonal skills, with the ability to engage and work with a range of stakeholders Desirable criteria 1.    Experience of working in the Higher Education sector and familiarity with HE administration 2.    Experience of developing graphics, videos and other digital content type 3.    Knowledge and experience of using Contensis Classic to manage webpages 4.    Knowledge and experience of using design software, including the Adobe Creative Suite and Canva 5.    Experience of Science Comms Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. Further Information We pride ourselves on being inclusive and welcoming. We embrace diversity and want everyone to feel that they belong and are connected to others in our community. We are committed to working with our staff and unions on these and other issues, to continue to support our people and to develop a diverse and inclusive culture at King's. As part of this commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion and through this appointment process, it is our aim to develop candidate pools that include applicants from all backgrounds and communities. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the advert. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘ How we Recruit ’ pages. Interviews are due to be held on Friday 20th November 2026. Apply now Apply now How we recruit At King’s we seek to recruit and retain skilled and engaged colleagues to deliver the competitive advantage. King’s benefits Our pay and benefits package attracts the best candidates from around the world. Creating meaningful impact Addressing urgent global challenges to shape a better world. Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link Alumni Footer navigation link Facilities Student services Footer navigation link Accommodation Footer navigation link Libraries & Collections Footer navigation link King’s IT Footer naviga