ink in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Arts & Humanities Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Law Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Nursing, Midwifery & Palliative Care Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Business Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Life Sciences & Medicine Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Natural, Mathematical & Engineering Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Dentistry, Oral & Craniofacial Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Social Sciences & Public Policy Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu About Navigation category. Contains links. Press enter key to expand. About Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu History Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Strategy Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Learning & Teaching Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Our People Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Work at King's Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Partnerships Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Governance, policies & procedures Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Professional & Support Services Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Diversity & Inclusion Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Financial information Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni King's College London Job id: 161445 . Salary: £39,755 - £44,685 pro rata per annum, including London Weighting Allowance . Posted: 23 October 2026 . Closing date: 29 October 2026 . Business unit: IoPPN . Department: Health Services & Population Research . Contact details: Sharli Paphitis . sharli.paphitis@kcl.ac.uk Location: Denmark Hill Campus . Category: Research . Back to job search THIS VACANCY IS OPEN TO INTERNAL APPLICANTS ONLY About us: The post will be based at King’s College London within the QUAHRC and the NIHR Research Support Service. The successful candidate will join a multidisciplinary team delivering an NIHR-funded methodology project focused on Collaborative Data Analysis (CDA) in applied health and social care research. Collaborative Data Analysis brings multiple perspectives into the interpretation of research data, including those of people with lived experience. While CDA has considerable potential to improve the relevance, inclusivity and usefulness of qualitative research, existing approaches are dispersed across methodological and participatory traditions and there is limited practical guidance to help research teams implement CDA well. The project will address this implementation gap by co-designing a national, integrated CDA implementation toolkit for applied health and social care research. The toolkit will include an implementation framework, methodological decision-support tool, training and facilitation resources, and an accessible digital platform. The project will work closely with researchers, methodological specialists, PPIE professionals and people with lived experience throughout its development. About the role: This is a part time post (18.75 hours per week), and you will be offered  a fixed term contract until 30/04/2028. Research staff at King’s are entitled to at least 10 days per year (pro-rata) for professional development. This entitlement, from the Concordat to Support the Career Development of Researchers, applies to Postdocs, Research Assistants, Research and Teaching Technicians, Teaching Fellows and AEP equivalent up to and including grade 7. Visit the Centre for Research Staff Development for more information. About you: To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria MSc in relevant subject area Strong knowledge and practical experience of qualitative research methods, including qualitative data analysis. Experience of conducting evidence synthesis, systematic/scoping reviews, methodological reviews, or other structured approaches to reviewing research literature. Excellent qualitative analytic and synthesis skills, including the ability to integrate information from different forms of evidence and identify patterns, concepts and methodological implications. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to develop respectful and effective working relationships with people from different professional, disciplinary and experiential backgrounds. Excellent organisational skills, with the ability to manage multiple activities, work independently, meet deadlines and maintain accurate research documentation. Knowledge or experience of collaborative data analysis or participatory approaches to qualitative data analysis. Experience of working sensitively with people or communities who may be underserved by, or marginalised within, conventional research processes. Desirable criteria Knowledge of a range of qualitative methodologies and their underlying epistemological or theoretical assumptions. Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. Further Information At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapidly, and we recognise its growing role in professional work. Applicants may use AI tools to support preparation of their application, for example to research the role or structure written responses. However, applications must reflect the applicant’s own work and experience. AI tools should not be used during interviews or assessment activities unless this has been agreed in advance as a reasonable adjustment. Further guidance on the use of AI in recruitment can be found here. We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. *We welcome your thoughts on how organisations can create an inclusive environment, including one which supports free speech and the exchange of a wide range of ideas. * To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘ How we Recruit’ pages. Apply now Apply now Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link Alumni Footer navigation link Facilities Student services Footer navigation link Accommodation Footer navigation link Libraries & Collections Footer navigation link King’s IT Footer navigation link Academic Calendar Footer navigation link Discover King's News Centre Footer navigation link Events Footer navigation link Student's Union Footer navigation link Visit King's Footer navigation link Work at King's Footer navigation link King's community Footer navigation link Contact us +44 (0)20 7836 5454 King's College London Strand London WC2R 2LS United Kingdom Terms & conditions Footer navigation link Privacy policy Footer navigation link Modern slavery statement Footer navigation link Site help Footer navigation link Accessibility Footer navigation link Manage cookies Footer navigation link"
