eturn to main menu Arts & Humanities Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Law Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Nursing, Midwifery & Palliative Care Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Business Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Life Sciences & Medicine Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Natural, Mathematical & Engineering Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Dentistry, Oral & Craniofacial Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Social Sciences & Public Policy Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu About Navigation category. Contains links. Press enter key to expand. About Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu History Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Strategy Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Learning & Teaching Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Our People Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Work at King's Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Partnerships Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Governance, policies & procedures Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Professional & Support Services Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Diversity & Inclusion Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Financial information Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni King's College London Job id: 161225 . Salary: £39,076 per annum inc London Weighting Allowance . Posted: 22 October 2026 . Closing date: 28 October 2026 . Business unit: Faculty of Life Sciences & Medicine . Department: St John's Institute of Dermatology . Contact details: Joanna Jackow-Malinowska . Joanna.jackow@kcl.ac.uk Location: Guy's Campus . Category: No category added . Back to job search About us The post will be based on the Guy’s Campus within the School of Basic and Medical Biosciences at St John’s Institute of Dermatology. The candidate will work closely with the principal investigator Dr Joanna Jacków-Malinowska . We will closely work with Prof John McGrath and Prof Jemima Mellerio, both of whom are world-renowned experts in Epidermolysis Bullosa and will be the key clinical personnel to guide with clinical relevant questions to this project. The postholder also will benefit more broadly from the vast interdisciplinary research and academic networks at King’s College London  and elsewhere. About the role We are seeking an enthusiastic researcher to join an innovative project investigating blister fluid as a minimally invasive source of patient-derived cells in recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa (RDEB). The project aims to establish a new liquid-biopsy approach for isolating and expanding keratinocytes, fibroblasts, stem/progenitor cells and mesenchymal stromal cells (MSCs) from EB blister fluid. The successful candidate will develop and optimise cell-isolation and culture protocols, characterise patient-derived cell populations using cell-surface markers, immunostaining and molecular/transcriptional approaches, and assess their potential as disease-relevant models for RDEB. The work will ultimately support the development of a patient-derived EB cell biobank and future gene-editing and regenerative therapies. This is an exciting opportunity to work at the interface of stem cell biology, dermatology, liquid biopsy and gene therapy, using patient-derived material to develop new approaches for understanding and treating EB. This is a full-time post (35 hours per week) based on-site. You will be offered a fixed term contract until 31st October 2027. Research staff at King’s are entitled to at least 10 days per year (pro-rata) for professional development. This entitlement, from the Concordat to Support the Career Development of Researchers , applies to Postdocs, Research Assistants, Research and Teaching Technicians, Teaching Fellows and AEP equivalent up to and including grade 7. Visit the Centre for Research Staff Development for more information. About you To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria . Master’s degree (or equivalent) in a relevant biological or biomedical science subject. Demonstrable research experience following completion of the Master’s degree keen in PhD afterwards. Practical experience working with primary human cells, ideally keratinocytes and/or fibroblasts. Experience in molecular and cell biology techniques and cell culture. Ability to plan and deliver experimental work independently to agreed timescales and quality standards. Good data-handling, analytical and scientific communication skills. Ability to work collaboratively across KCL St John’s Institute of Dermatology and other Institutes. Strong interest in EB, skin regeneration, patient-derived cells or gene-editing approaches, with enthusiasm to develop specialist expertise in EB blister-fluid-derived cells. Desirable criteria Experience working with epidermolysis bullosa (EB), wound healing or rare skin diseases. Experience handling blister fluid or other liquid-biopsy samples. Experience with mesenchymal stromal cells (MSCs) or other stem/progenitor cell populations. Experience with 3D skin models, organotypic cultures or regenerative medicine. Experience with gene-editing technologies, particularly in primary human skin cells. Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. Further Information At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapidly, and we recognise its growing role in professional work. Applicants may use AI tools to support preparation of their application, for example to research the role or structure written responses. However, applications must reflect the applicant’s own work and experience. AI tools should not be used during interviews or assessment activities unless this has been agreed in advance as a reasonable adjustment. Further guidance on the use of AI in recruitment can be found here . We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘How we Recruit’ pages. This post is subject to Disclosure and Barring Service and Occupational Health clearances. Apply now Apply now Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link Alumni Footer navigation link Facilities Student services Footer navigation link Accommodation Footer navigation link Libraries & Collections Footer navigation link King’s IT Footer navigation link Academic Calendar Footer navigation link Discover King's News Centre Footer navigation link Events Footer navigation link Student's Uni