 in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Arts & Humanities Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Law Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Nursing, Midwifery & Palliative Care Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Business Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Life Sciences & Medicine Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Natural, Mathematical & Engineering Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Dentistry, Oral & Craniofacial Sciences Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu Social Sciences & Public Policy Navigation link in category Our faculties. Press escape key to return to main menu About Navigation category. Contains links. Press enter key to expand. About Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu History Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Strategy Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Learning & Teaching Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Our People Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Work at King's Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Partnerships Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Governance, policies & procedures Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Professional & Support Services Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Diversity & Inclusion Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Financial information Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King’s Impact Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu King's Venues Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Sustainability Navigation link in category About. Press escape key to return to main menu Students Staff Alumni King's College London Job id: 161645 . Salary: Research Assistant £39,755 - 44,685 per annum, Including London Weighting Allowance Research Associate - £45,829 per annum, including London Weighting Allowance . Posted: 27 October 2026 . Closing date: 03 November 2026 . Business unit: IoPPN . Department: Health Services & Population Research . Contact details: Vanessa Lawrence . Vanessa.c.lawrence@kcl.ac.uk Location: Denmark Hill Campus . Category: Research . Back to job search ** THIS VACANCY IS OPEN TO INTERNAL APPLICANTS ONLY About us: ** The successful candidate will became a member of the Qualitative Applied Health Research Centre ( QUAHRC ) within the Health Services & Population Research Department, and will also be affiliated with the Centre for Affective Disorders, Department of Psychological Medicine, both within the Institute of Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience, King’s College London.  The post provides an exciting opportunity to join a thriving centre for qualitative research that uses rigorous and innovative methods to understand and improve the experiences of people with mental health problems.  The postholder will work closely with Professor Vanessa Lawrence, Professor in Qualitative Health Research and Head of the QUAHRC at Kings and Professor Cynthia Fu, Professor of Affective Neuroscience & Psychotherapy and Principal Investigator of BDEP. About the role: We invite applications for a Qualitative Research Assistant/Associate to contribute to a NIHR-funded clinical trial. The BDEP trial  aims to evaluate the efficacy, safety, and acceptability of home-based transcranial direct current stimulation (tDCS) for individuals with bipolar depression. BDEP includes a qualitative process evaluation to explore participants’ experiences of home-based tDCS, including how it affected their lives, reasons for adherence /non-adherence, how the intervention could influence outcomes, and how individual circumstances might influence their experiences. The primary purpose of this post is to support and undertake qualitative data analysis and interpretation for BDEP and to contribute to trial reports, publications and dissemination. Key responsibilities include: • Recruit participants within the clinical trial, liaising closely with the trial manager, research staff and clinical teams. •Conduct in-depth, sensitive qualitative interviews with people experiencing bipolar depression. •  Undertake analysis of qualitative data to develop an in-depth understanding of participants' expectations and experiences of home-based tDCS. •Undertake qualitative data management, coding and analysis for the BDEP qualitative process evaluation, working with the Chief Investigator, qualitative lead and wider study team. •  Attend and contribute to BDEP trial recruitment review meetings, presenting updates on research progress and qualitative findings to the wider study team. •  Maintain accurate and secure qualitative research records and ensure that data handling, analysis and reporting comply with the protocol, ethics approval, data-management requirements and relevant research governance. •  Prepare manuscripts for peer-reviewed journals and contribute to reports, conference presentations, seminars and other dissemination activities for academic, clinical and public audiences. •  Contribute actively to the vibrant research culture of the QUAHRC, bringing ideas and enthusiasm to its programme of research, methodological development and knowledge exchange through initiatives such as research group meetings, the Qualitative Special Interest Group, the Qualitative Open Mic podcast, the Qualitative Summer School, the Qualitative Impact Blog and other collaborative activities. This is a full-time post, and you will be offered an a fixed term contract for 7 months Research staff at King’s are entitled to at least 10 days per year (pro-rata) for professional development. This entitlement, from the Concordat to Support the Career Development of Researchers, applies to Postdocs, Research Assistants, Research and Teaching Technicians, Teaching Fellows and AEP equivalent up to and including grade 7. Visit the Centre for Research Staff Developmen t for more information. About you: To be successful in this role, we are looking for candidates to have the following skills and experience: Essential criteria Experience of conducting interviews with research participants with bipolar disorder Understanding the value of lived experience of people with mental health problems and those that support them Experience of recruiting participants to clinical trial research studies Demonstrable experience of qualitative data analysis, including systematic coding and development of themes Excellent written communication skills, including experience of contributing to high quality peer review publications or other research outputs Understanding of ethical and research governance requirements for mental health research, confidentiality, data protection Ability to work effectively and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary research team and to deliver work independently Desirable criteria Experience of qualitative research embedded within a clinical trial or process evaluation Experience of recruiting participants to clinical trial research studies Knowledge or experience of research concerning treatment acceptability, adherence, implementation or mechanisms of intervention effects Experience of analysing longitudinal qualitative data or interviews conducted at more than one time point Grade 6 Essentials  (in addition to the above criteria) PhD qualified in relevant subject area. Ability to contribute to the development of qualitative research approaches and methodological decisions, drawing on specialist knowledge of qualitative research methods. Experience of taking a lead role in preparing qualitative research outputs, including manuscripts for peer-reviewed publications, and communicating findings to academic and non-academic audiences. Downloading a copy of our Job Description Full details of the role and the skills, knowledge and experience required can be found in the Job Description document, provided at the bottom of the page. This document will provide information of what criteria will be assessed at each stage of the recruitment process. * Please note that the Grade 6 appointment is a PhD level role but candidates who have submitted their thesis and are awaiting award of their PhDs will be considered. In these circumstances the appointment will be made at Grade 5, spine point 30 with the title of Research Assistant. Upon confirmation of the award of the PhD, the job title will become Research Associate and the salary will increase to Grade 6. Further Information At King’s, we believe that the diversity of our community and a culture that is welcoming, open, inclusive and collaborative, are great strengths of the university. The Equality Act of 2010 protects the rights of our students and staff and provides a framework to fulfil our duties to eliminate unlawful discrimination, harassment and victimisation and in addition, to advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between those who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. At times, this will include balancing rights and beliefs that can feel in tension. We are committed to free speech and to academic freedom, believing that our foundational purpose as a university, is to create spaces where a wide range of ideas, including ideas that are controversial, can be discussed and debated, and where members of our community can express lawful views without fear of intimidation, harassment or discrimination. When engaging in the robust exchange of ideas, we ask that our community is mindful of our Dignity at King’s guidance. We ask all candidates to submit a copy of their CV, and a supporting statement, detailing how they meet the essential criteria listed in the person specification section of the job description. If we receive a strong field of candidates, we may use the desirable criteria to choose our final shortlist, so please include your evidence against these where possible. Artificial intelligence (AI) is evolving rapidly, and we recognise its growing role in professional work. Applicants may use AI tools to support preparation of their application, for example to research the role or structure written responses. However, applications must reflect the applicant’s own work and experience. AI tools should not be used during interviews or assessment activities unless this has been agreed in advance as a reasonable adjustment. Further guidance on the use of AI in recruitment can be found here. We reserve the right to close adverts early due to the volume of applications we receive. While the closing date may change, all adverts will close at 23:59 to allow sufficient time for applications to be submitted on that day. We encourage you to apply at the earliest opportunity to avoid disappointment as once we have closed a vacancy you will be unable to submit your application. To find out how our managers will review your application, please take a look at our ‘ How we Recruit’ pages. This post is subject to Disclosure and Barring Service and/or Occupational Health clearances. Apply now Apply now Connect with King’s College London Study Degree courses Footer navigation link Postgraduate taught Footer navigation link Postgraduate research Footer navigation link International Footer navigation link Summer schools Footer navigation link Student experience Footer navigation link Information for New students Footer navigation link Current students Footer navigation link Staff Footer navigation link Alumni Footer navigation link Facilities Student services Footer navigation link Accommodation Footer navigation link Libraries & Collections Footer navigation link King’s IT Footer navigation link Academic Calendar Footer navigation link Discover King's News Centre Footer navigation link Events Footer navigation link Student's Union Footer navigation link Visit King's Footer navigation link Work at King's Footer navigation link King's community Footer navigation link Contact us +44 (0)20 7836 5454 King's College London Strand London WC2R 2LS United Kingdom Terms & conditions Footer navigation link Privacy policy Footer navigation link Modern slavery statement Footer navigation link Site help Footer navigation link Accessibility Footer navigation link Manage cookies Footer navigation link"
